## Parallel Aggregation Pattern

```java
Executor ioExecutor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(20);

// Sequential: 3 calls × 100ms = 300ms total
// Parallel: all 3 run together = ~100ms total
CompletableFuture<UserDTO>   userFuture   = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> fetchUser(userId), ioExecutor);
CompletableFuture<OrderList> ordersFuture = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> fetchOrders(userId), ioExecutor);
CompletableFuture<Prefs>     prefsFuture  = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> fetchPrefs(userId), ioExecutor);

DashboardDTO result = CompletableFuture.allOf(userFuture, ordersFuture, prefsFuture)
    .thenApply(v -> new DashboardDTO(
        userFuture.join(),
        ordersFuture.join(),
        prefsFuture.join()
    ))
    .join();
```

## Chaining Operations

```java
// thenApply: transform result synchronously
CompletableFuture<String> name = CompletableFuture
    .supplyAsync(() -> fetchUser(userId), ioExecutor)
    .thenApply(User::getName);

// thenCompose: chain when transformation is also async (avoids CF<CF<T>>)
CompletableFuture<OrderDetails> details = CompletableFuture
    .supplyAsync(() -> fetchOrder(orderId), ioExecutor)
    .thenCompose(order -> CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> enrich(order), ioExecutor));

// thenCombine: merge two independent futures
CompletableFuture<Summary> summary = userFuture
    .thenCombine(ordersFuture, (user, orders) -> new Summary(user.getName(), orders.size()));
```

## Error Handling

```java
// exceptionally: fallback on failure
CompletableFuture<UserDTO> safe = CompletableFuture
    .supplyAsync(() -> fetchUser(userId), ioExecutor)
    .exceptionally(ex -> UserDTO.anonymous());

// handle: always runs — inspect result or error
.handle((user, ex) -> ex != null ? UserDTO.anonymous() : user)

// orTimeout (Java 9+): complete exceptionally after deadline
.orTimeout(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
.exceptionally(ex -> UserDTO.anonymous());
```

## Dedicated Thread Pool

```java
@Configuration
@EnableAsync
public class AsyncConfig {
    @Bean("ioExecutor")
    public Executor ioExecutor() {
        ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();
        executor.setCorePoolSize(10);
        executor.setMaxPoolSize(50);
        executor.setQueueCapacity(100);
        executor.setThreadNamePrefix("io-async-");
        executor.setRejectedExecutionHandler(new CallerRunsPolicy());
        executor.initialize();
        return executor;
    }
}
```

## Spring @Async

```java
@Service
public class ExternalService {
    @Async("ioExecutor")
    public CompletableFuture<UserDTO> fetchUserAsync(Long id) {
        return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(externalClient.getUser(id));
    }
}

// Use: call from another bean (proxy applies)
CompletableFuture<UserDTO> user = externalService.fetchUserAsync(userId);
```

## Proper Exception Handling

```java
public UserDTO fetchUserSafely(Long id) {
    try {
        return CompletableFuture
            .supplyAsync(() -> userClient.fetch(id), ioExecutor)
            .orTimeout(3, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
            .join();
    } catch (CompletionException ex) {
        Throwable cause = ex.getCause();
        if (cause instanceof TimeoutException) throw new ServiceTimeoutException("Timed out");
        throw new ServiceException("Failed to fetch user", cause);
    }
}
```

## synchronized vs volatile vs Atomic

```java
// synchronized: mutual exclusion + visibility
public class Counter {
    private int count = 0;
    public synchronized void increment() { count++; }
    public synchronized int get() { return count; }
}

// volatile: visibility only — wrong for increment!
public class BrokenCounter {
    private volatile int count = 0;
    // RACE: read-add-write is not atomic even with volatile
    public void increment() { count++; }
}

// Atomic: lock-free, correct
public class CorrectCounter {
    private final AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);
    public void increment() { count.incrementAndGet(); }
    public int get() { return count.get(); }
}
```

## ReentrantReadWriteLock — Concurrent Reads

```java
public class CachedData {
    private final ReadWriteLock lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();
    private Map<String, String> cache = new HashMap<>();

    public String get(String key) {
        lock.readLock().lock();   // multiple threads read simultaneously
        try { return cache.get(key); }
        finally { lock.readLock().unlock(); }
    }

    public void put(String key, String value) {
        lock.writeLock().lock();  // exclusive write
        try { cache.put(key, value); }
        finally { lock.writeLock().unlock(); }
    }
}
```

## Good volatile Use Case

```java
// volatile is correct for a single-write, many-read flag
public class Worker implements Runnable {
    private volatile boolean running = true;
    public void stop() { running = false; }  // write once
    public void run() {
        while (running) { doWork(); }  // read many times — sees fresh value
    }
}
```

## Enable Virtual Threads in Spring Boot 3.2+

```yaml
# application.yml
spring:
  threads:
    virtual:
      enabled: true  # Tomcat uses virtual threads per request
```

```java
// Or programmatically
@Bean
public TomcatProtocolHandlerCustomizer<?> virtualThreadsCustomizer() {
    return handler -> handler.setExecutor(Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor());
}

// @Async with virtual threads
@Bean("virtualThreadExecutor")
public Executor virtualThreadExecutor() {
    return Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor();
}
```

## Virtual Thread vs Platform Thread

```java
// Platform thread — backed by OS thread; limited to ~thousands
Thread platformThread = new Thread(() -> {
    // blocking here wastes an OS thread
    String result = httpClient.get(url);
});
platformThread.start();

// Virtual thread — JVM-managed; safe to block; millions possible
Thread virtualThread = Thread.ofVirtual().start(() -> {
    // JVM unmounts this during I/O; carrier thread stays free
    String result = httpClient.get(url);
});

// Create millions — no problem
try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
        executor.submit(() -> processRequest());
    }
}
```

## Structured Concurrency (Java 21 Preview)

```java
// All subtasks scoped to parent — if one fails, all cancelled
public DashboardDTO buildDashboard(Long userId) throws InterruptedException, ExecutionException {
    try (var scope = new StructuredTaskScope.ShutdownOnFailure()) {
        Subtask<UserDTO>   userTask   = scope.fork(() -> fetchUser(userId));
        Subtask<OrderList> ordersTask = scope.fork(() -> fetchOrders(userId));
        Subtask<Prefs>     prefsTask  = scope.fork(() -> fetchPrefs(userId));

        scope.join();           // wait for all
        scope.throwIfFailed();  // re-throw if any subtask failed

        return new DashboardDTO(
            userTask.get(),
            ordersTask.get(),
            prefsTask.get()
        );
    }
    // Scope exit: all subtasks guaranteed complete or cancelled
}
```

## Avoid synchronized with Virtual Threads

```java
// synchronized PINS virtual thread to carrier — reduces benefit
// BAD for virtual threads:
public synchronized void process() { /* ... */ }

// GOOD: use ReentrantLock instead
private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
public void process() {
    lock.lock();
    try { /* ... */ }
    finally { lock.unlock(); }
}
```

## Bulkhead Pattern — Separate Pools per Dependency

```java
@Configuration
public class ThreadPoolConfig {

    // CPU-bound work: image processing, data transformation
    @Bean("cpuExecutor")
    public Executor cpuExecutor() {
        int cpuCores = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors();
        ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();
        executor.setCorePoolSize(cpuCores);
        executor.setMaxPoolSize(cpuCores);
        executor.setQueueCapacity(500);
        executor.setThreadNamePrefix("cpu-");
        executor.initialize();
        return executor;
    }

    // I/O-bound: database calls
    @Bean("dbExecutor")
    public Executor dbExecutor() {
        ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();
        executor.setCorePoolSize(10);
        executor.setMaxPoolSize(30);
        executor.setQueueCapacity(200);
        executor.setThreadNamePrefix("db-");
        // CallerRunsPolicy: if pool saturated, caller thread does the work (backpressure)
        executor.setRejectedExecutionHandler(new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy());
        executor.initialize();
        return executor;
    }

    // I/O-bound: external HTTP APIs (separate from DB — bulkhead)
    @Bean("httpExecutor")
    public Executor httpExecutor() {
        ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();
        executor.setCorePoolSize(20);
        executor.setMaxPoolSize(50);
        executor.setQueueCapacity(100);
        executor.setThreadNamePrefix("http-");
        executor.initialize();
        return executor;
    }
}
```

## Little's Law in Practice

```
Scenario: Payment API calls average 200ms latency
Required throughput: 100 payments/second

Little's Law: Threads needed = throughput × latency
= 100 req/s × 0.2s = 20 threads minimum

With safety margin: core=25, max=40

Monitoring alert: if active threads > 35 → scale or investigate payment API latency
```

## Micrometer Metrics for Thread Pools

```java
@Bean
public ThreadPoolTaskExecutor monitoredExecutor(
        MeterRegistry meterRegistry) {
    ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();
    executor.setCorePoolSize(20);
    executor.setMaxPoolSize(50);
    executor.initialize();

    // Expose pool metrics to Prometheus/Grafana
    ExecutorServiceMetrics.monitor(
        meterRegistry,
        executor.getThreadPoolExecutor(),
        "http-executor",
        List.of(Tag.of("service", "payment-api"))
    );
    return executor;
}
// Metrics: executor.active, executor.queued, executor.pool.size, executor.completed
```

## CompletableFuture — Best For Parallel Aggregation

```java
// Clear and readable — fetch 3 things in parallel, combine
public DashboardDTO buildDashboard(Long userId) {
    var userF   = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> userService.find(userId), executor);
    var ordersF = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> orderService.recent(userId), executor);
    var prefsF  = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> prefService.get(userId), executor);

    return CompletableFuture.allOf(userF, ordersF, prefsF)
        .thenApply(v -> new DashboardDTO(userF.join(), ordersF.join(), prefsF.join()))
        .orTimeout(3, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
        .join();
}
```

## Project Reactor — Best For Streaming / Non-Blocking

```java
// WebFlux controller — non-blocking, streaming response
@RestController
public class EventController {

    // Server-Sent Events: stream events to client
    @GetMapping(value = "/events", produces = MediaType.TEXT_EVENT_STREAM_VALUE)
    public Flux<ServerSentEvent<String>> streamEvents() {
        return Flux.interval(Duration.ofSeconds(1))
            .map(i -> ServerSentEvent.builder("Event #" + i).build());
    }

    // Process stream with backpressure
    @PostMapping("/process")
    public Mono<ProcessResult> process(@RequestBody Flux<DataChunk> chunks) {
        return chunks
            .filter(chunk -> chunk.isValid())
            .flatMap(chunk -> processAsync(chunk), 10)  // max 10 concurrent
            .reduce(ProcessResult.empty(), ProcessResult::merge);
    }
}
```

## Decision Guide

```
Scenario                              → Choice
Parallel API calls, combine results   → CompletableFuture
Traditional REST service, JDBC DB     → Spring MVC + Virtual Threads (Java 21)
Streaming large dataset to client     → WebFlux + Flux
Real-time notifications (SSE/WS)      → WebFlux
Very high concurrency, R2DBC DB       → WebFlux
Mixed blocking and async code         → Spring MVC (easier to reason about)
```
